 ## Overview
-Shadow Ray Casting is a family of local illumination techniques that evaluate visibility between a point $\mathbf{p}$ in a volume and a light source by casting rays through the participating medium. It solves the problem of self-shadowing and occlusion in volumetric data, which standard ray-marching often ignores or approximates poorly. Unlike [Two-Pass Shadow Casting (Kajiya and Von Herzen)](2196-two-pass-shadow-casting-kajiya-and-von-herzen.md) which requires a separate light-space pass, or [Shadow Mapping](2934-shadow-mapping.md) which relies on depth buffers, this family directly integrates extinction along shadow paths during the primary rendering pass or via specialized acceleration structures.
+Shadow Ray Casting evaluates visibility from a light source to a point in a volume by integrating extinction along a ray, solving the problem of accurate volumetric shadowing where standard surface techniques fail due to participating media. It differs from [Two-Pass Shadow Casting (Kajiya and Von Herzen)](2196-two-pass-shadow-casting-kajiya-and-von-herzen.md) by computing shadows locally per-shading-point rather than via a global pre-pass, and from [Shadow Mapping](2934-shadow-mapping.md) by integrating physical transmittance rather than comparing discrete depth buffers.
 
