 ## Background
-Shadow ray casting in volume rendering evolved from surface ray tracing techniques. Early work by Kajiya and Von Herzen introduced two-pass shadow casting for volumes, establishing the need for light-space transmittance buffers [S1, unofficial]. As hardware capabilities grew, techniques like Shadow Mapping were adapted for volumes, though they often required significant performance overhead due to double traversal [S1, unofficial]. The introduction of Variance Shadow Mapping allowed for soft shadows in neural radiance fields (NeRFs) by leveraging statistical depth information [S1, unofficial]. More recently, lookup table methods and area light sampling have been integrated into real-time pipelines to balance physical accuracy with interactive performance [S1, unofficial].
+Shadow ray casting in volume rendering evolved from surface-based shadowing techniques to handle participating media. Early work by Kajiya and Von Herzen introduced two-pass methods for global illumination in volumes [2196-two-pass-shadow-casting-kajiya-and-von-herzen.md]. Local shadow casting became prevalent with the rise of real-time volume rendering, where per-pixel shadow rays provided accurate attenuation without the memory overhead of global shadow maps. The technique is widely used in medical visualization, atmospheric effects, and real-time game engines (e.g., VolPack, WebGPU voxel raymarchers) where physical accuracy of light transport through fog, smoke, or tissue is required.
 
